From 9f3fb39f5006eb57f63941eb39d45426edae0635 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 2 Jul 2026 21:59:14 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 05528/11362] [outer-loop] add live_llm_embedding_extraction
 substrate category, fix exp5178's false DURATION_TOO_SHORT

User: dug into the flag together, chose option 2 ('invest in the proper new category')
over patching exp5178's artifact alone.

exp5178's hidden-state verifier pilot loaded a real local GGUF model
(gemma-4-26B-A4B-it, Q4_K_M) via llama_cpp.Llama(embedding=True,
pooling_type=LAST).embed to extract final-token hidden-state vectors for 48
candidates, then trained a small centroid probe -- genuine compute, no
fabrication. Total wall-clock 35.28s is plausible for model load (~10-15s for
a 26B quantized model) plus 48 single-pass embedding extractions (no
iterative token-by-token decoding), but got CRITICAL-flagged under the
generic 60s live_llm_inference floor, which is calibrated for full
generative inference.

Investigated whether either existing 'cheap' substrate category fit:
verifier_ensemble_against_cached_candidates's definition explicitly requires
the LLM NOT be loaded at all (this task DID load it, for embeddings) --
neither existing category could honestly describe this workload.

Added live_llm_embedding_extraction as the 6th legal inference_substrate
value (LLM_EMBEDDING_EXTRACTION_SUBSTRATE, 2.0s floor -- calibrated as the
minimum plausible time to load any real local GGUF and complete at least one
embedding forward pass, low enough for genuine small-candidate-set work,
high enough to still catch a fabricated near-instant claim). Wired into
duration_floor_for_artifact() ahead of the generic live_llm_inference check
so a declared embedding-extraction substrate isn't pulled back to the 60s
floor by a GGUF path elsewhere in the artifact. Documented in CLAUDE.md's
Inference-Substrate Declaration Discipline table (6 legal values now) with
exp5178 as the canonical exemplar.

9 new tests (tests/python/test_adversarial_verify_llm_embedding_extraction_
substrate.py): recognition-function unit coverage, floor-priority-over-
generic-marker guard, an exact exp5178 reproduction now clean, a regression
guard confirming a genuinely-too-fast fabricated embedding claim is still
caught, and a regression guard confirming the new substrate doesn't become
a loophole for artifacts that actually claim full generation. Full suite
(254 tests) green. Corpus-wide dry-run (--backfill --high-precision-only,
4576 artifacts) found 7 qualifying unstamped artifacts -- a plausible small
count, no flood.

Also reclassified exp5178's own inference_substrate to the new value and
added the top-level model_specs/target_model fields the methodology check
requires (the model was previously only named in a nested field). Live
re-check: 0 flags (was 2 CRITICAL + 1 WARN). The artifact's substance is
unchanged: hidden_state_verifier_accuracy=0.0 (0/6, worse than tuned SC's
0.333), an honest but severely underpowered null at n=6 questions
(mcnemar_p=0.5) -- not a headline win, just no longer incorrectly quarantined.

